---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import provenance from '../data/provenance.json';
import photos from '../data/photos.json';
const photoList = Object.entries(photos);
---

<SiteLayout title="Every source has a seat"
  ><section class="page-intro">
    <p class="mono">Credits & sources</p><h1>Every source<br />has a seat.</h1><p>
      Food records, photography and the visual references behind this concept.
    </p>
  </section><article class="reading">
    <h2>The food.</h2><p>
      USDA Agricultural Research Service, <a href="https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/">FoodData Central</a>. The snapshot has {
        provenance.count
      } foods, per 100 g. USDA data is public domain / <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/"
        >CC0</a
      >. Sources: SR Legacy 2018 and Foundation Foods 2025. IDs and serving weights are preserved.
    </p><p>
      Packaged-food lookup uses <a href="https://world.openfoodfacts.org/">Open Food Facts</a>, whose database is
      licensed under <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/">ODbL</a>, with individual contents under
      DbCL. OFF-derived diary records retain this attribution in the local export. They are not merged into the CC0
      snapshot.
    </p><h2>The photographs.</h2><p>
      Every dish photo comes from Wikimedia Commons under an open license, resized and cropped to WebP at 480 and 960
      pixels. Each shows the dish it illustrates; the recipe's ingredient list defines the calculated nutrition.
      Share-alike derivatives remain under their original compatible license.
    </p><div class="credit-grid">
      {
        photoList.map(([image, photo]) => (
          <figure class="credit">
            <img src={`/busog/images/${image}-480.webp`} alt={photo.title} width="480" height="360" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>
              <a href={photo.source}>{photo.title}</a> by {photo.author}.{' '}
              {photo.licenseUrl ? <a href={photo.licenseUrl}>{photo.license}</a> : photo.license}.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))
      }
    </div><h2>The design.</h2><p>
      The owner-directed visual study is <a
        href="https://dribbble.com/shots/26787072-AI-Powered-Mobile-App-for-Restaurant-Operations"
        >AI-Powered Mobile App for Restaurant Operations by tubik</a
      >. Busog adapts the shell, card and typographic vocabulary with its own brand, artwork and nutrition interactions.
      No reference character art, photos or copy are reused.
    </p><p>
      <a href="https://nutrola.app/en">Nutrola</a> informed the page types.
    </p><p>
      Busog wrote its own copy. Self-hosted fonts are Barlow Condensed, IBM Plex Mono and DM Sans under SIL OFL; icons
      are Lucide under ISC.
    </p><h2>The boundaries.</h2><p>
      Mock history represents a fictional person. Photo recognition and coach replies are simulated. Food data is
      traceable, but a record or recipe composition cannot measure your actual restaurant portion. This concept makes no
      medical, user-count or outcome claims.
    </p>
  </article></SiteLayout
>
<style>
  .credit-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 28px 0 48px;
  }
  .credit img {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    border-radius: 18px;
  }
  .credit figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>
